WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:19.200
This is lesson five of MAD700, pointer P1. Activities, layouts, Intents, how you wire a screen in onCreate, the layout
attributes the exam still asks, mobile testing, and boundary value analysis from the study guide, pages 203 and 204.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:34.536
I'll show the real slides and the study guide, write the Java and XML on a board, then build a real app on a real
emulator. We'll break four exam traps, fix them, and finish with two TODO questions from the class test and a past paper.

00:00:34.536 --> 00:00:40.488
Aim for the paper on Tuesday the
24th of November, Lab 1, 9 o'clock.

00:00:40.488 --> 00:00:59.880
Open the Architecting Android Navigation deck. An Activity is the brain of one screen, a subclass of AppCompatActivity. You
never instantiate it yourself. A layout is the face, an XML tree of views. An Intent is the messenger that starts another screen.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:10.272
An explicit Intent names the class you want, inside your app. An implicit
Intent names an action, and Android finds an app that can handle it.

00:01:10.272 --> 00:01:20.040
If you start an Activity that is not listed in the manifest, the app
crashes. That is a favourite exam trap, and I will show it on a real run.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:35.232
Now the Wiring Android Logic deck. onCreate is where the screen is created. You always call super.onCreate
first, then setContentView to inflate the XML, and only after that do you call findViewById.

00:01:35.232 --> 00:01:47.328
If you reverse that order, findViewById returns null, and the next line that touches the widget
throws a NullPointerException. I will crash the app on purpose so you can see the dialog.

00:01:47.328 --> 00:01:57.096
Inside an anonymous click listener, this is the listener, not the
Activity. Write MainActivity.this when you build an Intent or a Toast.

00:01:57.096 --> 00:02:09.576
The lifecycle is onCreate, onStart, onResume, then onPause, onStop, and
onDestroy. The Back button typically resumes with onResume, not a fresh onCreate.

00:02:09.576 --> 00:02:22.128
Rotation destroys the Activity and creates a new one. If you have state that is not already in an
EditText, you save it in the Bundle with onSaveInstanceState, and you read it back in onCreate.

00:02:22.128 --> 00:02:31.608
Sample A question 1 and Sample B question 1 both test that you know
this. Do not just write that the app restarts — name the callbacks.

00:02:31.608 --> 00:02:46.512
P1 still asks layout attributes. match_parent fills the parent. wrap_content is
only as big as the content. For sizes use dp, for text use sp, never raw pixels.

00:02:46.512 --> 00:03:02.688
Padding is space inside the view. Margin is space outside it. gravity places the content inside this
view. layout_gravity places this view inside its parent. A FrameLayout defaults to the top left.

00:03:02.688 --> 00:03:20.640
In a LinearLayout, 0 dp plus layout_weight shares leftover space. Older study-guide pages still write
fill_parent; the current form is 0 dp. I will not redo ConstraintLayout, merge, or include — that was lesson four.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:30.312
The Blueprints deck is clear: an emulator cannot perfectly simulate sensors
such as GPS. Test on a real device as well, especially for hardware.

00:03:30.312 --> 00:03:39.552
To enable USB debugging, open About phone and tap the Build number
seven times, then turn on USB debugging in Developer options.

00:03:39.552 --> 00:04:00.672
Study-guide pages 203 and 204 call this fragmentation. You must test the minimum SDK, the target SDK, each screen size, and
different manufacturers. Chapter 1 still lists screen size and orientation, connectivity, battery, and hardware as testing issues.

00:04:00.672 --> 00:04:09.384
Page 203 is the only study-guide page on boundary value analysis,
and there is no lecturer slide for it, so learn this wording.

00:04:09.384 --> 00:04:24.672
The limits for each input should be identified, and each side of the limit should be tested. For a birthday, today
is displayed, and the next day is an error. For a telephone number, too many and too few digits are both tested.

00:04:24.672 --> 00:04:39.240
Worked the same way, a quantity of 1 to 99 is tested at 0, 1, 2, 98, 99, and 100.
Use greater-than-or-equal and less-than-or-equal so the limits themselves pass.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:50.568
On the board: MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity. In onCreate,
call super first, then setContentView, and only then findViewById.

00:04:51.018 --> 00:04:56.946
setOnClickListener takes an anonymous
listener. The tap handler is onClick.

00:04:57.396 --> 00:05:02.220
That is the wiring the class test
asks you to write, line by line.

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:11.478
An explicit Intent names the target class. Use
MainActivity.this, not this, because we are inside the listener.

00:05:11.928 --> 00:05:18.096
putExtra attaches the name and the quantity,
then startActivity opens the next screen.

00:05:18.546 --> 00:05:29.730
On ResultActivity, getIntent and getStringExtra or getIntExtra read those
values. The second argument of getIntExtra is the default if the key is missing.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:40.692
A vertical LinearLayout, padding 16 dp — padding is space
inside. The title is wrap_content, not match_parent.

00:05:41.142 --> 00:05:50.118
gravity centre horizontal places the text inside
this TextView. textSize is 22 sp, not pixels.

00:05:50.568 --> 00:05:57.336
Each button is 0 dp with weight 1, and
layout_marginTop is space outside the button.

00:05:57.786 --> 00:06:07.674
isQuantityValid returns true when the quantity is greater than or
equal to 1, and less than or equal to 99. The limits themselves pass.

00:06:08.124 --> 00:06:15.156
isPackedDateAllowed uses the study guide's birthday
rule: today is accepted, tomorrow is rejected.

00:06:15.606 --> 00:06:25.446
The six boundary values for 1 to 99 are 0
no, 1 yes, 2 yes, 98 yes, 99 yes, and 100 no.

00:06:25.896 --> 00:06:38.400
This is the real project, Pantry Basics. MainActivity wires the form after setContentView,
pre-fills today's packed date, and on Save it validates the quantity and the date.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:48.624
A valid save builds an explicit Intent to ResultActivity, with MainActivity.this,
and puts extras for the name, the quantity, and the packed date.

00:06:48.624 --> 00:07:03.768
Validator is a small helper, no widgets. isQuantityValid uses greater-than-or-equal and less-than-or-equal.
isPackedDateAllowed compares two ISO date strings, so today and any earlier date are allowed.

00:07:03.768 --> 00:07:08.472
The comment lists the six boundary
values the exam wants you to name.

00:07:08.472 --> 00:07:14.376
Gradle assembleDebug — BUILD SUCCESSFUL,
on this machine, with the real project.

00:07:14.376 --> 00:07:28.320
And here it is on a real Pixel 6, API 34. I type Rice, 12, leave the packed
date as today, the 21st of November 2026, hide the keyboard, and tap Save.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:36.336
ResultActivity reads the extras and
shows Rice x 12, packed 2026-11-21.

00:07:36.336 --> 00:07:46.344
Mistake one. I called findViewById before setContentView. Every
widget is still null, and the app stops as soon as it starts.

00:07:46.344 --> 00:07:53.232
Logcat: a NullPointerException, because
findViewById ran before the layout was inflated.

00:07:53.232 --> 00:07:58.248
Put setContentView first, rebuild,
and the form appears cleanly.

00:07:58.248 --> 00:08:10.416
Mistake two. I wrote greater than 1 and less than 99. I type Rice and
1, with today's date, and Save is rejected. The limit itself must pass.

00:08:10.416 --> 00:08:21.648
Change it to greater-than-or-equal and less-than-or-equal, type 1 again, and
the result screen opens: Rice x 1, packed today. The limit itself now passes.

00:08:21.648 --> 00:08:32.928
Mistake three. I left ResultActivity out of the manifest. The form
is valid — Rice, 12, today's date — I tap Save, and the app stops.

00:08:32.928 --> 00:08:45.048
Logcat: ActivityNotFoundException, Unable to find explicit activity class
ResultActivity. Have you declared this activity in your AndroidManifest.xml?

00:08:45.048 --> 00:08:54.120
Register ResultActivity in the manifest, rebuild, and the same valid
Save now opens the result screen, because the class is listed.

00:08:54.120 --> 00:09:03.336
Mistake four does not even install. I wrote new Intent(this,
ResultActivity.class) inside the anonymous listener.

00:09:03.336 --> 00:09:14.136
This is the real Gradle output: no suitable constructor for Intent, because
this is the OnClickListener, not a Context. It must be MainActivity.this.

00:09:14.136 --> 00:09:23.976
Write these down. setContentView before findViewById.
Inside a listener, write MainActivity.this, not this.

00:09:23.976 --> 00:09:36.144
Every Activity you start must be in the manifest. For boundary value analysis, test each side
of each limit, and keep the limits valid with greater-than-or-equal and less-than-or-equal.

00:09:36.144 --> 00:09:48.312
In the exam, write the explicit Intent with MainActivity.this and the class, then
startActivity. putExtra on the way out, getStringExtra or getIntExtra on the way in.

00:09:48.312 --> 00:10:03.840
Back resumes with onResume, not onCreate. Rotation destroys and recreates; use the
Bundle. dp for sizes, sp for text, padding versus margin, gravity versus layout_gravity.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:15.552
And for testing: min SDK, target SDK, each screen size, and different
manufacturers. Test sensors on a real device, not only the emulator.

00:10:15.552 --> 00:10:25.489
Your turn. Two questions, TODO style, like the class test. Pause,
write your Java, then I'll show the answers and a real run.

00:10:25.489 --> 00:10:45.313
Question 1 is CA Test question 1.2, and past-paper TODO question 1. Inside the click listener, create an explicit Intent from MainActivity
to ResultActivity, attach the name under the key name, and start the Activity. On ResultActivity, read that extra and show it.

00:11:05.313 --> 00:11:13.497
Question 2 is past-paper TODO question 5, on
study-guide page 203, in a Smart Pantry setting.

00:11:13.497 --> 00:11:27.753
Three TODOs: write isQuantityValid for 1 to 99 inclusive, write isPackedDateAllowed so today is accepted
and tomorrow is rejected, and list the six boundary values for the quantity, with accepted or rejected.

00:11:47.753 --> 00:12:03.689
Here are the answers. Question 1: new Intent, MainActivity.this, ResultActivity.class, putExtra
name, startActivity. On the other screen, getIntent, getStringExtra name, and setText.

00:12:03.689 --> 00:12:22.889
Question 2: isQuantityValid is greater-than-or-equal 1 and less-than-or-equal 99. isPackedDateAllowed accepts
today and rejects a later date. The six values are 0 rejected, 1 and 2 accepted, 98 and 99 accepted, 100 rejected.

00:12:22.889 --> 00:12:40.481
And a real run of those answers. The report lists 0 rejected, 1 and 2 accepted, 98 and 99
accepted, 100 rejected. Today, the 21st of November, is accepted; tomorrow, the 22nd, is rejected.

00:12:40.481 --> 00:12:52.337
Then I open the form, type Rice and 12, tap Save, and the
extras arrive on ResultActivity: Rice x 12, packed 2026-11-21.

00:12:52.337 --> 00:13:02.153
That's lesson five, and the last of the planned reteach videos.
The exam is Tuesday the 24th of November, 9 o'clock, Lab 1.

00:13:02.153 --> 00:13:07.961
Write the two questions on paper, run the
project, and send me photos of your work.
